# GRE Practice Test 2 (held out of the drills). Original items. Keys first (see build.py).

STIMULI = [
    {"id": "GRE-T2-P1", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "When the first public lending libraries opened in British industrial towns in the 1850s, their founders described them as "
        "instruments of improvement: workers who read history and science, it was hoped, would become sober, skilled and content. Borrowing "
        "records from the period show that readers had other ideas. Fiction made up well over half of all loans at most libraries, and "
        "serialized adventure stories were among the most requested titles. Some library committees responded by limiting their purchases of "
        "novels; others decided that any reading was better than none and bought what was asked for. The records thus document not only what "
        "workers read but a running negotiation between the people who ran the libraries and the people who used them.")},
    {"id": "GRE-T2-P2", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "Most mammals stop producing lactase, the enzyme that digests the sugar in milk, soon after weaning. In some human populations, "
        "however, lactase production continues into adulthood, a trait known as lactase persistence. Genetic studies have found that the trait "
        "arose independently in several regions, through different mutations, in each case among peoples with a long history of herding "
        "cattle, goats or camels. The pattern is a textbook example of convergent evolution: similar pressures produced similar outcomes by "
        "different routes. What the pressure was remains debated. Milk supplies calories, fat and protein, but in some dry regions it may also "
        "have been valued as a source of clean water, and researchers disagree about which benefit mattered most.")},
    {"id": "GRE-T2-P3", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "Urban planners once assumed that wider roads would ease congestion. Experience suggested otherwise. Within a few years of a major "
        "highway's expansion, traffic often returned to its earlier levels, a pattern known as induced demand. The extra capacity made driving "
        "faster for a while, and people responded by driving more: taking trips they would otherwise have skipped, choosing more distant jobs "
        "or homes, or switching from buses and trains to cars.\n\n"
        "The same logic can run in reverse. When a road is closed or narrowed, some of its traffic does not reappear elsewhere but simply "
        "vanishes, as people combine errands, travel at other times or stop making certain trips. Planners in several cities have used this "
        "finding to justify converting traffic lanes into bus lanes or space for pedestrians, with less disruption than critics predicted.")},
    {"id": "GRE-T2-P4", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "The octopus has a large nervous system for an invertebrate, but most of its neurons are not in its brain. About two thirds lie in its "
        "eight arms, which can carry out complex movements with little direction from the center: an arm separated from the body will still "
        "reach for food and pass it toward where the mouth would be. Researchers describe the arrangement as distributed control. The brain "
        "sets general goals, such as reaching for a crab, while each arm works out the details of how to bend and grasp.")},
    {"id": "GRE-T2-P5", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "The standard history of photography treats its early decades as a contest between art and science. In one camp stood those who saw "
        "the camera as a mechanical recorder of facts, useful to astronomers and surveyors; in the other, those who insisted that a photograph "
        "could be composed, lit and printed with as much intention as a painting. The story ends, conventionally, with the triumph of the "
        "second camp and photography's admission to museums.\n\n"
        "Recent scholarship has questioned whether the two camps were ever as distinct as this account suggests. Many of the same practitioners "
        "produced both scientific plates and exhibition prints, and techniques moved freely between the two: methods developed for "
        "photographing faint stars, for example, were adapted by portraitists seeking softer light. The rivalry, on this view, was largely a "
        "creation of later critics, who needed a narrative in which photography had to earn its status as art. That need says more about the "
        "critics' own debates than about the medium's early practice.")},
    {"id": "GRE-T2-P6", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "Because volcanoes release carbon dioxide, one might expect eruptions to warm the planet. Large explosive eruptions, however, can cool "
        "it for several years. They inject sulfur gases high into the stratosphere, where the gases form tiny droplets that reflect sunlight "
        "before it reaches the surface. Because the stratosphere has no rain to wash the droplets out, they can linger for two or three years, "
        "far longer than particles in the lower atmosphere. The carbon dioxide from such an eruption, by contrast, is small compared with what "
        "human activity releases each year, so its warming effect is minor.")},
]

